PLACES=[
N('st-louis-cathedral-new-orleans','St. Louis Cathedral','New Orleans','la','worship','standing',29.95800,-90.06375,'exact','view','walk','','1789–',
  'The seat of the Archdiocese of New Orleans, on Jackson Square. Churches have stood on the spot since 1718; the second burned in the great fire of Good Friday 1788. The third was begun in 1789, finished in 1794 and made a cathedral in 1793; a central tower with a clock and a bell named "Victoire", for the Battle of New Orleans, was added in 1819. Between 1849 and the 1850s it was enlarged and largely rebuilt to plans by J. N. B. de Pouilly, and little of the 1789 church is left.',
  'The white three-spired front facing Jackson Square, between the Cabildo and the Presbytère, at the head of the pedestrian block of Chartres Street. Interior not checked.',
  'The oldest cathedral in continuous use in the United States, by one count.',
  accn='An active cathedral of the Archdiocese of New Orleans. Its own pages refused to open when this was written, so Mass and visiting times are not checked. Jackson Square and the street in front are public; parking not checked.',
  links=[W('St. Louis Cathedral (New Orleans)')],
  brick='?'),
N('cabildo-new-orleans','The Cabildo','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.95767,-90.06381,'exact','open','walk','','1795–',
  'The Spanish town hall on Jackson Square, rebuilt in 1795 to 1799 after the first one burned in the fire of 1788. The transfer of the Louisiana Purchase was signed here in 1803; the American eagle replaced the Spanish arms on the pediment in 1821, and the mansard third floor was added in 1847. The Louisiana Supreme Court sat here from 1868 to 1910, and Plessy v. Ferguson was heard in its main hall. It has been part of the Louisiana State Museum since 1911. A fire in 1988 destroyed the cupola and the third floor; it was restored and reopened in 1994.',
  'The arcaded building on the upriver side of the cathedral, at 701 Chartres Street, with the eagle in the pediment. Inside is the museum of Louisiana history; the Sala Capitular, the old council and court room, is on the second floor.',
  'Where Louisiana became American, and where Plessy was heard.',
  accn='Owned by the State of Louisiana and run as part of the Louisiana State Museum. Admission $15 for adults, $13 for Louisiana residents, free for children 6 and under, last tickets at 3:30 p.m.; hours from the museum\'s own page, read November 23, 2026. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('The Cabildo'),['Louisiana State Museum: the Cabildo',LSM+'cabildo']],
  brick='?',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Mondays in summer and on some state holidays'),
N('presbytere-new-orleans','The Presbytère','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.95824,-90.06333,'exact','open','walk','','1791–',
  'Designed in 1791 by Gilberto Guillemard to match the Cabildo on the other side of the cathedral, on the site of the Capuchin friars\' house. Only the ground floor was done by 1798 and the upper floor in 1813. It was never used to house priests: it was let out for business, then held the Louisiana Supreme Court from 1834. The city bought it in 1853, the state in 1908, and it joined the Louisiana State Museum in 1911. The mansard roof dates from 1847; its cupola, lost in the hurricane of 1915, was put back in 2005.',
  'The twin of the Cabildo, on the downriver side of the cathedral at 751 Chartres Street: a two-storey brick building with a nine-arch open arcade and a dormered mansard roof with a cupola. Inside is a museum of the state museum system.',
  '',
  accn='Owned by the State of Louisiana and run as part of the Louisiana State Museum. Admission $15 for adults, $13 for Louisiana residents, free for children 6 and under, last tickets at 3:30 p.m.; hours from the museum\'s own page, read November 23, 2026. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('The Presbytere'),['Louisiana State Museum: the Presbytère',LSM+'presbytere']],
  brick='?',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Mondays in summer and on some state holidays'),
N('old-ursuline-convent-new-orleans','Old Ursuline Convent','New Orleans','la','worship','standing',29.96085,-90.06066,'exact','open','walk','','1745–',
  'The second convent of the Ursuline nuns, who came from Rouen in 1727 to run a hospital and teach girls. Their first building, of exposed half-timber, was decaying by 1745, and this one, of stuccoed brick to plans by Ignace François Broutin, was finished by 1753. The nuns moved downriver in 1824 and the building became the bishop\'s house, later called the Archbishop\'s Palace. The National Park Service calls it the finest surviving example of French colonial public architecture in the country; by some accounts it is the oldest building in New Orleans.',
  'A plain, symmetrical two-storey front with a hipped roof and small dormers, at 1100 Chartres Street, with a gatehouse of about 1825 to 1830. A winding cypress stair with an iron handrail, thought to come from the first convent, is in the entrance hall. It is a museum now.',
  'The oldest building in the city, from the French years.',
  accn='Run as the Old Ursuline Convent Museum by the Archdiocese of New Orleans. The museum\'s own website could not be reached when this was written, and listings elsewhere disagree on its days, so hours are not checked: look them up before going. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Old Ursuline Convent, New Orleans')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('us-custom-house-new-orleans','U.S. Custom House','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.95211,-90.06634,'exact','view','walk','','1848–1881',
  'A granite-faced federal building filling a whole block of Canal Street, begun in 1848 to the design of Alexander Thompson Wood and finished only in 1881 after eight more architects and the Civil War. It held the custom house, the main post office and the federal courts; during the war it was used by both armies and held up to 2,000 captured Confederate soldiers. The Audubon Insectarium was inside from 2008 to 2023. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1974.',
  'Four storeys of Quincy granite over brick, with Egyptian-style columns in the middle of each front, empty statue niches on the ground floor and a rounded corner at Canal and North Peters streets, at 423 Canal Street. Inside, the Marble Hall, a Greek Revival room with a skylight, is one of the finest of its kind in the country; when the public may see it was not checked.',
  'Thirty-three years to build.',
  accn='A federal building with U.S. Customs offices. Seen from Canal, Decatur, Iberville and North Peters streets; whether the public may go in was not checked. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('United States Custom House (New Orleans)')],
  brick='no'),
N('gallier-hall-new-orleans','Gallier Hall','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.94857,-90.07136,'exact','view','walk','','1845–1853',
  'The old city hall of New Orleans, designed by James Gallier Sr., begun in 1845 and dedicated on May 10, 1853. It was city hall for just over a century, until the offices moved to Duncan Plaza in the 1950s. It is still used for civic events: mayors are sworn in here, distinguished citizens lie in state here, and on Mardi Gras Day the mayor toasts the kings of Zulu and Rex from its front. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1974.',
  'A three-storey marble Greek Revival front with two rows of fluted Ionic columns, at 545 St. Charles Avenue facing Lafayette Square. The building is booked for events and holds a youth theatre; the venue\'s page gives no hours for visitors.',
  'Where Mardi Gras stops to toast the mayor.',
  accn='Owned by the City of New Orleans and run as an event venue. St. Charles Avenue and Lafayette Square in front are public. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Gallier Hall'),['Gallier Hall (venue page)','https://gallierhall.com/']],
  brick='no'),
N('confederate-memorial-hall-new-orleans','Confederate Memorial Hall','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.94343,-90.07130,'exact','open','walk','','1888–',
  'A museum hall completed in 1888 to designs by Sully & Toledano, in the Richardsonian Romanesque style of the Howard Library next door, and given in 1891 by Frank T. Howard to the Louisiana Historical Association for its collections. On May 29, 1893 the body of Jefferson Davis lay here, mourned by more than 60,000 people, before being moved to Richmond. It calls itself Louisiana\'s oldest museum and holds over 5,000 Civil War objects. Its ownership was fought over in court for decades.',
  'The Romanesque hall at 929 Camp Street, between the two buildings of the Ogden Museum of Southern Art. Inside are uniforms, flags and personal effects of Confederate soldiers and officers.',
  '',
  accn='A private museum. Hours from its own page, read November 23, 2026; it is also closed on major holidays. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Confederate Memorial Hall Museum'),['Confederate Memorial Hall Museum','https://www.confederatemuseum.com/']],
  brick='yes',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Sunday, Monday and major holidays'),
N('hibernia-bank-building-new-orleans','Hibernia Bank Building','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.95199,-90.07149,'site','view','walk','','1921–',
  'A 23-storey, 355-foot office tower completed in 1921 for the Hibernia Bank, the tallest building in Louisiana until the new state capitol took the title in 1932. The bank left its offices from 2006, keeping only the branch in the lobby, and the building is being turned into apartments and offices. The white tower on top once served ships on the river as a beacon, and is lit in colours on holidays.',
  'The tower at the corner of Gravier and Carondelet streets (812 Gravier Street), with the white tower on its top that is part of the city skyline.',
  'The tallest building in Louisiana for eleven years.',
  accn='Privately owned. Seen from Gravier and Carondelet streets. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Hibernia Bank Building (New Orleans)')],
  brick='?'),
N('hotel-monteleone-new-orleans','Hotel Monteleone','New Orleans','la','hotel','standing',29.95421,-90.06769,'exact','open','walk','','1886–',
  'A family-run hotel begun in 1886, when Antonio Monteleone, a cobbler from Sicily, bought a small hotel at Royal and Iberville streets and then the Commercial Hotel. It was renamed Hotel Monteleone in 1908 and grew in 1903, 1908, 1928, 1954 (when the original building was pulled down and rebuilt) and 1964. Its Carousel Bar, a 25-seat bar that turns once every 15 minutes, opened in 1949. Hemingway, Tennessee Williams and Faulkner stayed here, and it was named a literary landmark in 1999.',
  'The tall hotel at 214 Royal Street, the only high-rise in the inner French Quarter, with the revolving Carousel Bar inside.',
  'A bar that goes round once every fifteen minutes.',
  accn='A working hotel, owned by the Monteleone family. The hotel\'s page gives no hours for the bar; look them up before going. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Hotel Monteleone'),['Hotel Monteleone','https://www.hotelmonteleone.com/']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('roosevelt-hotel-new-orleans','The Roosevelt (Hotel Grunewald)','New Orleans','la','hotel','standing',29.95428,-90.07239,'site','open','walk','','1893–',
  'Opened by Louis Grunewald in December 1893 as the Hotel Grunewald, on the site of his music hall. A 14-storey annex opened on January 1, 1908, with a nightclub called The Cave, built to look like a cavern with waterfalls and stalactites. New owners renamed it The Roosevelt in 1923, for Theodore Roosevelt, and replaced the first building with a 16-storey tower in 1925. Its Blue Room, opened on the last night of 1935, was for decades the city\'s leading music hall. It is now run by Waldorf Astoria.',
  'The hotel on Roosevelt Way, with its block-long lobby, decorated every Christmas since the 1920s.',
  'A nightclub built as a cave, under a hotel.',
  accn='A working hotel. Hours for its bars and lobby not checked. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('The Roosevelt New Orleans')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('saenger-theatre-new-orleans','Saenger Theatre','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.95639,-90.07258,'exact','open','walk','','1927–',
  'The flagship of Julian and Abe Saenger\'s chain of picture palaces, opened on February 4, 1927 with 4,000 seats. Emile Weil designed the hall as an Italian Baroque courtyard under a night sky, with 150 lights set out as constellations. It was cut into two cinemas in 1964, reopened as a performing arts centre in 1980, and was flooded a foot over the stage by Hurricane Katrina in 2005. After a $53 million restoration it reopened on September 27, 2013. Its Robert Morton organ of 1927, of about 2,000 pipes, is still there.',
  'The theatre at 1111 Canal Street, with its marquee on Canal and its box office on Rampart Street. The starry ceiling and the courtyard hall are seen by going to a show.',
  'A ceiling of stars, back after Katrina.',
  accn='Owned by the Canal Street Development Corporation, a city agency, and leased to the theatre\'s operators. Inside by ticket for a show; box office hours not checked. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Saenger Theatre (New Orleans)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('orpheum-theater-new-orleans','Orpheum Theater','New Orleans','la','civic','standing',29.95454,-90.07283,'exact','open','walk','','1918–',
  'A Beaux Arts vaudeville house designed by G. Albert Lansburgh, built in 1918 and opened in 1921, with 1,500 seats in a tall "vertical hall" laid out for sight lines and sound before amplifiers. It was to be pulled down in 1983 but was saved by the city\'s symphony, and became home of the Louisiana Philharmonic Orchestra in 1991. Flooded by Katrina in 2005, it stood empty until a $13 million renovation; it reopened in 2015 and the orchestra played again from September 17, 2015.',
  'The theatre at 129 Roosevelt Way. Inside by going to a concert or event.',
  '',
  accn='Privately owned. Inside by ticket; box office hours not checked. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Orpheum Theater (New Orleans)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('st-alphonsus-church-new-orleans','St. Alphonsus Church','New Orleans','la','worship','standing',29.92988,-90.07412,'exact','open','walk','','1857–',
  'The church of the Irish Catholics of the Lower Garden District, completed in 1857 across Constance Street from the German church of St. Mary\'s Assumption. It is one of the few richly coloured church interiors in the country from before the 1870s. The parish was closed in 1979 and the building was taken over by a community group in the 1980s; it is now the St. Alphonsus Art and Cultural Center. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1996.',
  'A two-towered Italianate front with pilasters, corbelled brick panels and statues in niches, at 2029 Constance Street. Inside: a coved ceiling painted by Dominique Canova, Bavarian stained glass by F. X. Zettler and mosaic floors.',
  'Two churches face each other across one street: one Irish, one German.',
  accn='Run as a cultural centre by a community group. No page of its own giving visiting days could be found when this was written, so hours are not checked. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('St. Alphonsus Church (New Orleans, Louisiana)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('st-marys-assumption-church-new-orleans','St. Mary\'s Assumption Church','New Orleans','la','worship','standing',29.92953,-90.07449,'exact','open','walk','','1860–',
  'Built for the German Catholic immigrants of the Lower Garden District and completed in 1860, across the street from the Irish St. Alphonsus. A rare and elaborate German Baroque Revival church, it was made a National Historic Landmark in 1974. Hurricane Betsy damaged it so badly in 1965 that it was nearly pulled down, and Katrina damaged the plaster and ceilings in 2005. Father Francis Xavier Seelos, its pastor, died of yellow fever in 1867 after nursing its victims; he was beatified in 2000, and a shrine and museum to him are here.',
  'The church at Constance and Josephine streets, with the Seelos Shrine at 919 Josephine Street. Two pipe organs: the grand organ in the second balcony, built in 1861 by Simmons & Willcox of Boston, and a Dutch choir organ of 1971 brought here in 2015.',
  '',
  accn='An active church, served by the Redemptorists. Mass times and shrine hours are from the Seelos Shrine\'s own page, read November 23, 2026. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('St. Mary\'s Assumption Church (New Orleans, Louisiana)'),['Seelos Shrine','https://www.seelos.org/']],
  brick='?',hours='Mass Monday to Friday 11:30 a.m.; shrine Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('st-patricks-church-new-orleans','St. Patrick\'s Church','New Orleans','la','worship','standing',29.94667,-90.06991,'exact','open','walk','','1838–1840',
  'The second-oldest Catholic parish in New Orleans, founded in 1833 for the English-speaking Irish of Faubourg St. Mary, the "American Quarter". The church was finished in 1840 to the design of James H. Dakin, and served as the pro-cathedral while St. Louis Cathedral was rebuilt from 1849 to 1851. It is one of the earliest Gothic Revival churches in the country, and was made a National Historic Landmark in 1975. It stood on high ground in Katrina and held one of the first Masses in the city afterwards, on October 2, 2005.',
  'A plain front and a 185-foot bell tower at 724 Camp Street; inside, an 85-foot nave with fan vaulting, sixteen stained glass windows in a half-dome over the altar and three large paintings behind it.',
  '',
  accn='An active Catholic church. Mass times from the parish\'s own page, read November 23, 2026; it lists no other visiting hours. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('St. Patrick\'s Church (New Orleans)'),['Old St. Patrick\'s: Mass times','https://oldstpatricks.org/mass-times/']],
  brick='?',hours='Mass Monday to Friday 7:15 and 11:30 a.m.; Saturday 8 a.m. and 5:30 p.m.; Sunday 8, 9:15 and 11 a.m. and 5:30 p.m.'),
N('jackson-brewery-new-orleans','Jackson Brewery (Jax Brewery)','New Orleans','la','industry','standing',29.95592,-90.06309,'site','open','walk','','1890–',
  'The Jackson Brewing Company, founded by Lawrence Fabacher in 1890, made Jax beer here; with Dixie it was one of only two New Orleans breweries to survive Prohibition, and in the 1950s it was the largest brewery in the South. It was sold to Pearl in 1974, which brewed Jax until 1984. The brewery building in the French Quarter was then turned into shops, restaurants and homes, and since 2023 it has held the studios of the radio station WWOZ.',
  'The old brewery building at 600 Decatur Street in the French Quarter, with shops inside.',
  'The biggest brewery in the South, now a mall.',
  accn='Privately owned; the shops are open to the public. Hours from the Shops at Jax Brewery\'s own page, read November 23, 2026. Parking not checked.',
  links=[W('Jackson Brewing Company (New Orleans)'),['The Shops at Jax Brewery','https://www.shopsatjaxbrewery.com/']],
  brick='?',hours='Sunday to Thursday 10 a.m. to 7 p.m.; Friday and Saturday 10 a.m. to 8 p.m.'),
]
